# Getting Ready for the Curriculum

This professional learning module gives you a guided preview of the key concepts and instructional approaches used throughout Unit 1: Logic-Based AI.

Think of this module as a head start: a chance to build familiarity with the ideas your students will encounter, and to start thinking about how you'll teach them in your classroom.

This module highlights important concepts and teaching strategies, but it doesn't replace reviewing individual lesson materials. Take time to review each lesson before teaching it with students.

<div style="clear:both;"></div>

---

## What's in this module?

<div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 20px; max-width: 900px; margin: 25px auto;">

  <div style="background: #FFFFFF; border: 1px solid #E4E2F8; border-left: 6px solid #4C42CF; padding: 18px 20px; border-radius: 10px; box-shadow: 2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
    <h3>Section 1 - Getting Started</h3>
    <div style="display: inline-block; background-color: #E4E2F8; color: #1F1976; font-size: 13px; font-weight: bold; padding: 4px 10px; border-radius: 10px; margin-bottom: 8px;">You are here</div>
    <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;">A short orientation to what this module covers and how to move through it, plus where Unit 1 fits into the year-long course and the AI-901 certification.<br><br><em>10 minutes</em></div>
  </div>

  <div style="background: #E4E2F8; border-left: 6px solid #4C42CF; padding: 18px 20px; border-radius: 10px; box-shadow: 2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
    <h3>Section 2 - What Is AI, Really?</h3>
    <div style="font-size: 13px; color: #1F1976; margin-bottom: 6px;">Aligns to curriculum Lessons 1-2</div>
    <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;">Before diving into code, students need a working definition of intelligence itself. You'll preview the sorting activity that surfaces "AI is many approaches, not one," plus a condensed history walkthrough grounding AI in linguistics, math, and psychology.<br><br><em>15 minutes</em></div>
  </div>

  <div style="background: #FFFFFF; border: 1px solid #E4E2F8; border-left: 6px solid #4C42CF; padding: 18px 20px; border-radius: 10px; box-shadow: 2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
    <h3>Section 3 - Python Basics</h3>
    <div style="font-size: 13px; color: #555555; margin-bottom: 6px;">Aligns to curriculum Lessons 3-5</div>
    <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;">Pseudocode, variables, and functions: this is where students write their first lines of Python. You'll walk through the same syntax-error triage and defining-vs-calling distinctions students commonly need help with early on.<br><br><em>30 minutes</em></div>
  </div>

  <div style="background: #E4E2F8; border-left: 6px solid #4C42CF; padding: 18px 20px; border-radius: 10px; box-shadow: 2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
    <h3>Section 4 - Optimization in Action</h3>
    <div style="font-size: 13px; color: #1F1976; margin-bottom: 6px;">Aligns to curriculum Lesson 6</div>
    <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;">Optimization sounds abstract until students see it in action. This section previews the hill-climbing simulation and unplugged thermostat activity that make the concept concrete, along with the local-vs-global-optimum distinction students often miss.<br><br><em>20 minutes</em></div>
  </div>

  <div style="background: #FFFFFF; border: 1px solid #E4E2F8; border-left: 6px solid #4C42CF; padding: 18px 20px; border-radius: 10px; box-shadow: 2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
    <h3>Section 5 - Loop Basics</h3>
    <div style="font-size: 13px; color: #555555; margin-bottom: 6px;">Aligns to curriculum Lessons 7-8</div>
    <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;">For loops and while loops are really one skill taught across two lessons. You'll step through the same loop executions your students will trace, and preview the off-by-one and infinite-loop mistakes to watch for.<br><br><em>20 minutes</em></div>
  </div>

  <div style="background: #E4E2F8; border-left: 6px solid #4C42CF; padding: 18px 20px; border-radius: 10px; box-shadow: 2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
    <h3>Section 6 - Reasoning and Its Limits</h3>
    <div style="font-size: 13px; color: #1F1976; margin-bottom: 6px;">Aligns to curriculum Lessons 9-12</div>
    <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;">This section traces an arc: reading logic, building logic, then questioning logic. You'll preview how students debug real error messages, translate decision trees into code, and confront where rule-based AI breaks down, setting up why machine learning exists in Unit 2.<br><br><em>25 minutes</em></div>
  </div>

  <div style="background: #FFFFFF; border: 1px solid #E4E2F8; border-left: 6px solid #4C42CF; padding: 18px 20px; border-radius: 10px; box-shadow: 2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
    <h3>Section 7 - Bringing It Together</h3>
    <div style="font-size: 13px; color: #555555; margin-bottom: 6px;">Aligns to curriculum Lesson 13</div>
    <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;">Students apply the whole unit in one project: choosing a topic, drawing a decision tree, then implementing it in Python. You'll preview the rubric and the most common failure mode, a working program whose decision tree doesn't reflect meaningful branching, so you're ready to coach a student through it.<br><br><em>15 minutes</em></div>
  </div>

</div>

<br>

**Total suggested time:** 135 minutes

---

## Engaging with This Module

As you move through the module, focus on building confidence with the concepts, noticing instructional strategies, and making connections to how students may experience the learning in your classroom.

Throughout these lessons, you'll also see how these concepts connect to the **AI-901 certification** your students are working toward.
